#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "c427d9b4-2944-5edc-8e19-363de348d23d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15147" :question/problem "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\n![](images/7e/7e7fbe6aaf1310b186344a3cf5a127075f11a86b51db2e687ddc7035d5d890f8.png)" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C\\mathbin:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nThe outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a3d9864bb6bada1fd218b299427bc5bb4ffcbcccb1a504fff77416d5d292151.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/93/9310d17389f61de2984c0e23cfe1060e77d4a87a140d2f1c892bdbab9b99629b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179483 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for Flux"}] :question/id #uuid "15426c37-1508-5407-ab49-b26f8761795c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15148" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 8y\\,\\mathbf i + 4x\\,\\mathbf j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^2+y^2 = 4.$ If the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n![](images/70/707f56c886072458eefdd7bcee2328992caa214b4f4954ba59c92bc972b66409.png)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf n = \\dfrac x2\\,\\mathbf i + \\dfrac y2\\,\\mathbf j.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(8y\\,i+4x\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4xy+2xy\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}6xy\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $6xy.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179484 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179646 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"}] :question/id #uuid "f4a82102-6dd6-5a73-8370-9050e0f2323a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15149" :question/problem "![](images/05/05c91d681a0c84bbe9d8b6cab9bd8cdf1dca8bf88c851b8c7a7045f73ff9844c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3,$ and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179485 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "446c5e28-00d3-5a17-ad90-0e66b5ec3a59" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Rectangle Using Two Symmetry Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "3f592794-e0e9-529c-9040-7b2806de2380" :question/math-academy-id "e-15150" :question/problem "![](images/59/59b5f1676f036f80e6aa2e9e331327ed9ec28e88bacda52a91ee0865736e9ce3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 5x\\,\\mathbf i - 3y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$ \n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/79/793755cda5243dbb427d8722c21ab150a4821c3957f0ccd0ef0fedbbd576e924.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(5x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(5x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10859d25-668f-5816-af25-2f8f651c2adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177856} {:answer/id #uuid "25ee1056-9705-59ee-9a75-4c87721c080a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177857} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca5ffeb-70c8-5dd6-ad43-d4b589b2f907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177858} {:answer/id #uuid "51f614c5-97b9-5cf0-9215-eafa949f0a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177859} {:answer/id #uuid "67764856-b008-565b-9f53-6d0005aa71c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177860}] :answer-field/id #uuid "45251c46-c4eb-5ef4-a338-46c73974a207" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177855}] :question/id #uuid "a2d693ae-0e11-561b-9b69-4ee74bfcc078" :question/math-academy-id "q-126726" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n2. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n3. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive"}]}
